<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">28 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 28 April, from 357 to 1969.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">357</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Constantius II makes his only visit to Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Emperor Constantius II entered Rome in triumph for the first time. The historian Ammianus Marcellinus described his astonishment at the Forum of Trajan and other monuments.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1789</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Fletcher Christian leads the mutiny on the Bounty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Mutineers seized HMS Bounty and set Captain William Bligh adrift with 18 men in an open launch. Bligh navigated some 6,700 km to Timor with the loss of only one man.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1818</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Rush-Bagot Agreement is proclaimed</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">President Monroe proclaimed the Anglo-American agreement limiting warships on the Great Lakes and Lake Champlain. It helped demilitarise the border between the United States and British North America.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1945</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Partisans shoot Mussolini near Lake Como</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Benito Mussolini and Clara Petacci were shot by partisans at Giulino di Mezzegra. The next day their bodies were hung upside down in Milan's Piazzale Loreto.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1952</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Japan regains its sovereignty as the Allied occupation ends</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of San Francisco came into force, ending the Allied occupation of Japan that had begun in 1945. A US-Japan security treaty took effect the same day.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1969</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles de Gaulle resigns as President of France</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After voters rejected his referendum on regional and Senate reform, de Gaulle stepped down at noon. He had led the Fifth Republic since its foundation in 1958.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a></div></li></ol>
